---
// THE SIDE MARKER on an index row: the figure plus the word. Three call sites (the two index
// matrices and LockedRow), so the marker can't drift between them.
//
// IT IS THE MARK'S ONE JOB ON THE PAGE NOW (chooser: andrewshiau-mark-options, Q3/05 — "as the
// row marker: it does a job"). The 44px figure that used to sit above the name in the pinned
// panel is gone, and `Mark.astro` went with it. The reasoning was the ask: how little of the
// mark do you want on the page? A masthead asserts identity to a reader who already knows whose
// site they are on; the same figure in the marker column tells them which half of the practice
// each of eight rows belongs to. So the mark appears where it carries information, and the
// panel opens on the name.
//
// ONE FIGURE, THREE STATES, and they are the mark's own vocabulary rather than three drawings:
//   both  — the whole figure: hollow ring, rule, solid disc.
//   design — the ring alone.
//   build  — the disc alone.
// Which is why this now draws `MarkFigure` instead of its own glyph. It used to render a FLAT
// 22×8 version of the figure for `both` and a CSS `::before` dot for the other two, hue being
// the only thing separating them — three hand-drawn marks for one idea, and the two that
// mattered most were distinguishable only by colour. The ring/disc pair replaces the hue
// (see MarkFigure.astro), and once the figure had a state per side there was nothing left for a
// second drawing to do. The flat glyph existed because a 22×8 lozenge sits on a meta line's
// baseline where a 26px square doesn't; at 18px — see `.side .mark` in global.css — the square
// fits inside the meta line's own 18px leading, so the reason is spent.
//
// THE WORD IS ALWAYS EMITTED, and on the index it is clipped rather than absent — `.sw` is
// sized to nothing by `.entry .em .side .sw` in global.css, which keeps it in the accessibility
// tree and in the `title` below. Eight rows repeating "Design + Build" was the loudest thing in
// a column meant to annotate them (chooser: andrewshiau-panel-marks, Q1/05). The ring/disc
// distinction survives greyscale where the old two hues did not (navy and olive are 2.14:1
// apart — STYLE.md), so the glyph is now a real signal rather than decoration over a hidden
// one; the word stays anyway, because a shape a reader has to learn is not a label. A `span`,
// not a bare text node, because you cannot address a text node from CSS.
//
// `title` on the wrapper, so a sighted reader who wants the word can hover for it. It is the
// one affordance replacing what the visible word did.
import { SIDE_LABEL, type Side } from '../data/experiments';
import MarkFigure from './MarkFigure.astro';
interface Props { side: Side }
const { side } = Astro.props;
---
<span class={`side side-${side}`} title={SIDE_LABEL[side]}>
  <MarkFigure side={side} />
  <span class="sw">{SIDE_LABEL[side]}</span>
</span>
